#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516139b1-0c44-5a73-bbcd-74968842ba7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346208} {:answer/id #uuid "a7404818-5591-58aa-8264-23893dddbbe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346209} {:answer/id #uuid "0e11e5f5-ffb7-5b65-b310-ece8db299f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346210} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4c77b6-c609-5c2e-98d7-3491780ec88b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346211} {:answer/id #uuid "97beeffd-ec10-5cef-9ccc-8c7dac3b6ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97beeffd-ec10-5cef-9ccc-8c7dac3b6ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346212} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d73fc8-e01b-525a-8920-6d15cbb9db6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd94ef21-a2a4-56ba-aab3-62f25fd6ae09" :question/math-academy-id "q-119774" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/d1e4f48450a7f2032d755a5255d1a1e8a726dd16d273dae5dc1e587826d5ce58.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bca84bd9-d547-5030-869f-ffb3283b2c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171392} {:answer/id #uuid "bef9fbd7-8042-536c-9b9a-6a69324bbe02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171393} {:answer/id #uuid "c3883ec6-b4a9-5671-a4b0-c328735d1b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171394} {:answer/id #uuid "f46b6f4d-ef42-5812-8931-ece5f0837f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171395} {:answer/id #uuid "4ce2cdff-6c14-5a3d-a789-7635cb493366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3883ec6-b4a9-5671-a4b0-c328735d1b6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171394} :answer-field/id #uuid "26dfc9f1-5feb-5fc7-b3b9-e5500ae396a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd7005de-da4a-5aae-a0ea-f0c01c178438" :question/math-academy-id "q-96767" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=2xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/6ef852c9c15dc6f591119906abe479949f929edb6dc26f21588b4de545d02da2.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\\,\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\\,\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\\,\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d408b37-044f-54cd-a51a-928a075e0d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346215} {:answer/id #uuid "eb53295e-af49-5343-b6f5-84fba66b3325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346216} {:answer/id #uuid "99dc08f8-6370-5619-adb9-b2240ec54d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346217} {:answer/id #uuid "9cde33d4-299d-5e9c-9692-35abea4ae8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346218} {:answer/id #uuid "109290c1-fc89-5ffb-afe3-ec9f9cdf0221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb53295e-af49-5343-b6f5-84fba66b3325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346216} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aa91f6d-b7a5-56fe-a6a3-90200d467108" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9207fabe-2c1e-5522-9989-db0098e7f899" :question/math-academy-id "q-97534" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x-1)(y-1)^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y>1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x-1)(y-1)^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=1$ or $y=1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/075264fa042adc64e160a83f03a2b125dd5a9b0927fc3ed318778c0004689ec4.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/5716d59a745fc148b504a4db523286d7c7209fe793fcf369a90d220125f7e9fb.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $x<1$ and $y<1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $x>1$ and $y<1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x>1$ and $y>1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b90fa3f-a7c8-5111-9f0b-292024a034ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/cb58d805bed05b6846fd0ab7657f8e05c65eb69562a6cda494689e44745fb9d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346222} {:answer/id #uuid "aae5858c-c671-58a8-99d9-e4122fd25837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/4ea9eceec3de9b414efa87954d869119caf0748f152186aff5ede9bbade3f1fc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346223} {:answer/id #uuid "edf2ad87-441d-5cc6-9c51-29f68e4bf4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/d94451671073d38af905b1190a15f97e6419a3a6052f83e53caa5a94b4a7000a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346224} {:answer/id #uuid "0a85b9ba-5486-5832-b1b9-dc61080679f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/7428d46543d8dd2def3e48b980643572dd661f5f7edc6cba304d64eb99439403.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346225} {:answer/id #uuid "733f6a92-b3df-5452-886d-de94acf65329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/2c71db516bb0ab1514fe6e8979c980210c396fcafb88fd566ae9754ad0792bc8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aae5858c-c671-58a8-99d9-e4122fd25837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/4ea9eceec3de9b414efa87954d869119caf0748f152186aff5ede9bbade3f1fc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346223} :answer-field/id #uuid "adf7a2cd-078b-5100-b3d9-11599b6eb692" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "785c04b6-0c68-5e88-b598-3068a3e40899" :question/math-academy-id "q-98229" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $y$ given a fixed value of $x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/4ea9eceec3de9b414efa87954d869119caf0748f152186aff5ede9bbade3f1fc.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/fe2169790c1ee1d823937235bedd20ff10ecca662f030fd03dbfe23434517e47.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6db2e9cc-38b0-5392-8ab8-8a2783ef1e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/43d4b05227a0667420e6978f01671931fb2392fe39692400407708edc8c24987.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346229} {:answer/id #uuid "47c191ca-ff91-5452-a5ca-18d833cc9c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/ab73df79e9518f68e27860a1655867d092255e604666c4153f3d950b30410fc5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346230} {:answer/id #uuid "abb66116-6683-521e-adc6-59b58fbf2933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/58a69833c7b4f627c0cfd436f5ff624ea15dfc0f68d548e94b987794f06a58a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346231} {:answer/id #uuid "79a72175-246d-56b0-a5e1-4fdb90548e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/4191f22560c393dacad29a06e34b7c725800e27554e2c4ad7a9e40d9e56741e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346232} {:answer/id #uuid "c47fef2f-f072-55fb-8ccb-f6303e5aa769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/e45b8a64f56d831392ddc1eca4ddec922949d58e1c3f1f69c4db6238fe9a3200.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db2e9cc-38b0-5392-8ab8-8a2783ef1e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/43d4b05227a0667420e6978f01671931fb2392fe39692400407708edc8c24987.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346229} :answer-field/id #uuid "7805ad67-4eba-5e87-81f5-06cc7d5df2d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6e290eb2-4c30-594d-9303-2ef73ece0ff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-98231" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $x$ given a fixed value of $y.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/43d4b05227a0667420e6978f01671931fb2392fe39692400407708edc8c24987.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063607/assets/718f65dc7f4ccab0931d04855c647bd39beffc014f1f389896edf83352fcc988.png)"}]}
